  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis depicts a 598 dam H5 high centered over New\nMexico with attending mid/upper ridging extending northward all\nthe way into Alberta, with an upper trough extending from \nOntario into the Upper Midwest. This continues to yield \nnorthwesterly flow aloft over the region. At the surface, a very\nrelaxed pressure gradient has resulted in calm winds, and with \nsolid moisture return between yesterday afternoon and this \nmorning pulling dew points back to the upper 60s to lower 70s \nand saturating the boundary layer, patchy to dense fog has \ndeveloped across the region. The most dense fog, where \nvisibility is on the order of quarter to half mile, is across \nfar NW Missouri, which has prompted the issuance of a dense fog \nadvisory. Fog should mix out by around 9 am this morning. By \nlater today, a surface low associated with the upper trough \nacross the Upper Midwest will move eastward across Ontario, with\na trailing cold front moving northwest to southeast across the \nCWA. This will yield a bit of a gradient for high temperatures \ntoday, with high temperatures in the mid 80s across northern MO \nwhere the front will move through later this morning and highs \nin the upper 80s to lower 90s further south where the front will\narrive a bit later this afternoon. Drier air will filter in \nbehind the front, with dew points projected to fall to the mid \n60s by this evening. For tomorrow, seasonal temperatures are \nexpected, but with dew points in the upper 50s it should be a\nrelatively pleasant day and evening. Temperatures may increase \na degree or two on Friday, but humidity should remain \nrelatively comfortable. \n\nBy this weekend, the upper ridge axis is progged to drift \nfurther east as an upper trough comes ashore across the Pacific \nNorthwest (and will also flatten out the ridge). This should \nlead to increased mid level heights and warmer 850 temps, \ntranslating to surface high temperatures into the lower to mid \n90s for Saturday. By Sunday and Monday, the center of the H5\nhigh shifts eastward over the region. The upper trough will make\nits way across the Intermountain West and into the Northern\nPlains, with southerly low level flow increasing across our CWA\nin response and ushering in some moisture return. Highs on\nSunday are forecast to reach the mid 90s for most locations, and\nhighs in the lower to mid 90s on Monday. However, recent trends\nin the guidance has lowered temps and dew points a bit, and the\ncurrent grids reflect max heat indices on Sunday and Monday in\nthe mid to upper 90s (rather than 100 to slightly above 100 this\ntime yesterday). Shower and thunderstorm chances return to the\nforecast Sunday night into Monday morning (20 to 40%) and again\nMonday night into Tuesday morning (20 to 50%).",
